Simplify Path
Problem Description

Given an absolute Unix-style file path that begins with a slash /, convert it into its simplified canonical path.

In a Unix-style file system:

. refers to the current directory.
.. refers to the parent directory.
Multiple consecutive slashes (//) are treated as a single slash.
Any other sequence of characters represents a directory name.

The simplified path must:

Start with a single /.
Have no trailing slash unless it is the root directory.
Have no . or .. directory references.
Approach: Stack

The solution uses a stack to process the path components and maintain the canonical directory structure.

How It Works
Split the input path using / to get individual components.
Initialize an empty stack.
Iterate through each component:
If the component is empty or ., ignore it.
If the component is .., remove the top directory from the stack if it is not empty.
Otherwise, push the directory name onto the stack.
Join the remaining stack elements using /.
Add a leading / to construct the simplified canonical path.

The stack ensures that parent directory references are handled correctly without moving above the root directory.

Example

Input:

python
path = "/home//foo/../bar/"


Output:

python
"/home/bar"


Explanation:

/home adds home to the stack.
// is ignored.
foo is added to the stack.
.. removes foo.
bar is added to the stack.
The trailing slash is ignored.

The final simplified path is /home/bar.

Complexity Analysis

Let N be the length of the input path.

Time Complexity: O(N) — Splitting, processing, and joining the path components take linear time overall.
Space Complexity: O(N) — The components list and stack may store up to O(N) characters.
